File        .github/workflows/cve-remediation.yml
Base blob   f8d9b8b, the file on the default branch on 2026-08-21
Milestone   M4b, task 8, bean fiddle-amgc

This repository cannot run this patch. It targets a repository this project does
not own, and the run needs a Wiz credential, a Jira credential and a model key.
So the patch and the reasons below are the whole record.

WHAT CHANGES

One step goes away: `Run claude-code-action for CVE remediation`, which uses
`anthropics/claude-code-action@v1`. Four steps take its place.

  1. Download fiddle and verify it before it runs
  2. Build the docker image fiddle scans
  3. Run fiddle for CVE remediation
  4. Map fiddle's verdicts to the Jira contract

The `remediate` job's `permissions:` block also gains one line, `checks: read`.
That is the second hunk, and the section below says why.

The checkout step gains one line, `persist-credentials: false`. The section
"THE CHECKOUT MUST PERSIST NO CREDENTIAL" says why, and that line is not a hunk
here for the reason stated there.

Every other step in the `remediate` job is byte-identical. The patch is two
hunks. It removes eight lines, and all eight belong to the deleted step. These
stay: `needs: scan`, the `if` on `has_work` and `on_default_branch`,
`actions/checkout@v4` with `fetch-depth: 0`, the `cve-lists` download, and
`setup-wiz`. The three permissions the job already held stay, and a fourth joins
them. The Jira step and the Slack step stay. The `scan` job is untouched.

THE CHECKOUT MUST PERSIST NO CREDENTIAL

Add `persist-credentials: false` beside `fetch-depth: 0` in the checkout step.

fiddle authenticates every git operation that reaches a remote. It offers one
`http.https://github.com/.extraHeader` to the fetch and to the push (ADR 046).
`actions/checkout` persists a header of its own by default. Both headers then
apply to the same request, and the remote answers
`remote: Duplicate header: "Authorization"`. Run 32533261303 failed that way.

Removing fiddle's header is not the alternative. With `persist-credentials:
false` and no header of its own, the fetch fails with
`could not read Username for 'https://github.com'`. Run 32578049819 failed that
way.

This line is prose and not a diff hunk. A hunk needs three lines of trailing
context, and this record does not hold the three lines that follow the checkout
block. A fabricated context would produce a patch that does not apply.

`setup-wiz` stays because it installs `wizcli` and logs it in. fiddle uses the
login that action leaves behind. fiddle names no configuration directory of its
own, and it gives the credential to no scanner. A run without that step reaches
the scanner and gets exit 1 with no report.

The `cve-lists` download stays because the Jira step still reads `to-fix.json`.
That step links the run's pull request on a ticket for a CVE that left the
verdict list. fiddle ignores both lists.

The job needs no `git config` step. fiddle passes its own committer to every
commit it makes.

THE JOB NEEDS A FOURTH PERMISSION, AND ALL FOUR ARE NAMED

A permission is not a step, so the byte-identical claim above does not cover it.
This says the change in the one place a reviewer of the pull request will read
it, and the diff carries the same list as a comment above the block.

  contents: write        push the remediation branch, read refs
  pull-requests: write   open the shared pull request, read and write its body
  id-token: write        the job already held this; nothing fiddle does spends it
  checks: read           read the check runs on the candidate commit

`checks: read` is the added one. `observe_genuine_failure` spends it on
`GET /repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}/check-runs`, which is how a later night learns
what CI concluded about the commit an earlier night pushed, and so whether to
attempt the shared pull request again. Neither `contents` nor `pull-requests`
implies `checks`, and a `permissions:` block sets every scope it does not name to
none, so the grant has to be written. `docs/technical/cve-repository.md` records
the same four for `FIDDLE_CVE_TOKEN` against the disposable target, and names
`Checks` as the one that is easy to miss.

`id-token: write` is listed for completeness rather than because fiddle asks for
it. It was already on the job before this patch, no step this patch adds spends
it, and this patch does not remove it.

Without `checks: read` the read is refused with a 403, and a refused read is not
a pass. fiddle makes no attempt, publishes the row `checks_unreadable` naming the
endpoint and the commit it asked about, and exits 11. So the job fails, the
verdict mapping step below is skipped, `non-patchable.json` stays absent and the
Jira step exits early — the same shape a failed model step had. It does not
report a night with nothing to do.

THE CHECKSUM IS VERIFIED BEFORE THE BINARY RUNS

Two steps, in this order: one verifies, a later one executes. The download step
never executes what it downloads. A failed verification fails the job, and the
run step does not start.

The step compares the measured digest with two values.

  - The checksum published beside the asset, `fiddle-linux-amd64.sha256`. This
    catches a truncated download and a mismatched pair of assets.
  - `FIDDLE_SHA256`, a digest this workflow pins. This catches a re-cut tag.

The second comparison is the one the design asks for, and the first cannot do
that work. The design says a tag alone lets a re-cut tag silently change what a
scheduled job runs. A re-cut tag republishes the binary and its checksum
together. The published checksum then agrees with the new binary, so a check
against it passes and reports nothing. Only a digest held outside the release
detects the change.

`FIDDLE_TAG` and `FIDDLE_SHA256` move together. The step says so by name when
they disagree. Both are placeholders in this patch, because `peel/fiddle`
publishes no release yet. `FIDDLE_SHA256` reads
`replace-with-the-sha256-the-tag-publishes`, which no digest matches, so a
workflow applied without an edit fails on its first run and says what to do.

`FIDDLE_TAG` holds the whole tag, `v0.1.0` and not `0.1.0`. fiddle's release
workflow fires on the tag filter `v*`, so the prefix is part of the name. The
step joins that tag to the download URL and builds no version string from a
number. A package version in a scan carries no such prefix, and nothing in this
patch reads one.

`peel/fiddle` is public, so `curl` needs no credential. The download goes to
`$RUNNER_TEMP`, not the checkout, so the binary cannot reach a commit.

THE IMAGE

fiddle scans an image tag. It does not build one. The deleted step built the
image inside a model turn, which is why its allowlist carried
`Bash(docker build *)`. The build is a step now. It writes `icecube:scan`, the
tag the `scan` job builds, and `[orchestration.cve] image` names the same tag.

THE ECOSYSTEM'S OWN TOOLS

The deleted step ran `go` inside a model turn, which is why its allowlist
carried `Bash(go *)`. fiddle needs an entry of its own. `[[workspace.commands]]`
in `fiddle.toml` names each program an attempt may run and the arguments it
takes. A program the document does not name is refused. There is no interpreter,
so `sh -c` is refused for the same reason `curl` is: no declaration names it.
`[workspace] command_timeout` bounds each run, and the child gets `HOME`,
`PATH`, `LANG` and one toolchain locator and no credential.
`docs/technical/decisions/043-a-deployment-declares-the-programs-an-attempt-may-run.md`
records the decision.

So a repository whose repairs need `go mod tidy` writes that down. fiddle names
no ecosystem and ships no default, and an attempt against a document that
declares nothing gets four tools instead of five. The attempt still declares
every file it changed, `go.sum` included, because knowing that a bump implies a
checksum file is the agent's knowledge and not fiddle's.

THE ENTRY IS NARROWER IN SURFACE AND NOT IN AUTHORITY, AND THE JOB IS WHAT
ISOLATES

`Bash(go *)` let the model compose any `go` command line. `[[workspace.commands]]`
lets it append to a prefix the document fixed. That is a smaller surface and a
legible one, and it is not a smaller authority: `go build` and `go test` compile
and run code out of the repository, so either allowlist amounts to arbitrary
execution under the runner's identity. Nothing in fiddle confines it. Decision
043 states the four things that do hold.

This is safe here because of where it runs. The `remediate` job is a disposable
GitHub-hosted virtual machine that is destroyed after the run, and the same was
true of the step this replaces. That property belongs to the workflow rather than
to fiddle, which is why it is written down here. An operator who moves this
capability to a long-lived self-hosted runner, or who runs `fiddle run` on a
laptop, loses it and gets nothing in its place.

The declared programs also give the attempt a network path it directs itself.
`go mod tidy` fetches from a module proxy, so the runner needs egress to it. The
deleted step needed the same egress for the same reason.

THE VERDICT MAPPING

fiddle writes `verdicts.json` into `[report] dir`, and `write_report` is the only
writer. The Jira step reads `non-patchable.json` as an array of
`{cve, severity, verdict, package, rationale}`. One jq program maps one to the
other.

  [ .[]
    | select(.legacy_label != null)
    | { cve, severity, verdict: .legacy_label, package, rationale }
  ]
  | unique_by(.cve)

`verdict` reads `legacy_label`. It does not read fiddle's own `verdict` field.
The Jira step builds the label as `cve-<verdict>`, and the JQL in the `scan` job
closes `cve-upstream-blocked` and `cve-needs-work`. fiddle's `verdict` field
carries its disposition name, which is `needs_work` with an underscore. That
label closes nothing, and a ticket carrying it would stay open forever.

`select(.legacy_label != null)` drops a row that has no label. `Row` answers
`legacy_label` with `None` for six of its eight values, and only
`unsafe_without_direction` and `pull_request` answer with a label. A row without
one has no meaning in Jira, and mapping it would file `cve-null`. `nothing_to_do`
and `checks_unreadable` are both among the six, which is why the row that says a
run could not read CI files no ticket.

No package type crosses this step. The Jira contract is five fields and none of
them is `packageType`, and a `Verdict` carries no package type either. So the
`scan` job's `OS` and fiddle's `os` never meet. A sixth field would need a case
map rather than a copy.

`unique_by(.cve)` matches `to-fix.json` and `upstream-blocked.json`. One CVE in
two packages becomes one ticket.

`severity` passes through unchanged. fiddle writes `HIGH` and `CRITICAL`, which
is what the Jira contract asks for, because `[orchestration.cve] severities`
defaults to those two grades. A deployment that adds `MEDIUM` sends `MEDIUM` to
the ticket summary.

The mapping step reads `[report] dir` from `fiddle config check --json`, through
a step output. The path lives in the document and not in the workflow.

The mapping step carries no `if: always()`. A failed run skips it, so
`non-patchable.json` stays absent and the Jira step exits early. That is what the
job does today when the model step fails. On a completed run the file is always
there, so the step fails loudly when it is missing.

THE TWO COUNTS DIFFER, AND THE WORKFLOW SAYS SO

The `scan` job keeps a finding in `to-fix.json` only when `fixedVersion` is not
null, and it sends the rest to `upstream-blocked.json`, away from remediation.
M4c deleted that partition from fiddle. fiddle sends a finding with no published
fix to the agent as well, and the agent declines it with its own reason.

So the two report different totals for one image, and neither is wrong. The
comment above the run step says this in the file an operator reads. A comment in
fiddle would be in the wrong repository, and this repository permits none.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT CARRY

`fiddle.toml` at the root of the host repository. The workflow depends on three
things in it: `[orchestration.cve] image` names `icecube:scan`, `[report] dir`
names a directory the repository ignores, and the credential tables name
`FIDDLE_GITHUB_TOKEN`, `LITELLM_API_KEY`, `WIZ_CLIENT_ID` and
`WIZ_CLIENT_SECRET`.

`[agent] base_url` writes the gateway URL. The endpoint is not a credential and
the host repository is private, so the document carries the value and this step
exports no variable for it. ADR 040 also permits `{ env = "NAME" }`, which a
deployment whose endpoint is sensitive can use instead; a named variable that
nothing exports is refused with exit 2. The document also needs `[scanner] cli` to read
`{ program = "wizcli", args = ["scan", "container-image"] }`, because fiddle adds
`--by-policy-hits=DISABLED`, the output path and the image, and nothing else.
`[workspace]` names what judges a repair. The skill this patch stops using ran
`go build ./...`, `go fmt ./...`, `go vet ./...`, a `docker build` and a rescan.
Which of those the host declares is the host's decision. This lane cannot make
that decision correctly, so it does not write the document.

`[github] base` replaces `CVE_BASE_BRANCH`. The deleted step read the default

