same label. `ticket_proposals` skips a verdict whose `legacy_label` is `None`,
which is `select(.legacy_label != null)`. It keeps the first row for each CVE and
skips the rest, which is `unique_by(.cve)`. It writes the label
`cve-<legacy_label>` from `TICKET_LABEL_PREFIX`, which is the `cve-<verdict>` the
Jira step builds. `cve`, `severity` and `package` reach the summary, and
`rationale` reaches the description. Nothing has run the jq program and a fiddle filing over
one verdict list and compared the two sets of tickets, so this is a reading of
two programs and not a measurement.

So the retirement rests on a measured effect, a stub-measured run path and an
argued contract. It does not rest on a binary that has filed a real ticket,
because none has. What would settle that gap is one run of a `fiddle` binary
against a real site, reading a `fiddle.toml` that carries `[jira.filing]`.
M5b did not take it, and this record does not claim it.

WHAT THE HOST MUST ADD BEFORE IT REMOVES THE MAPPING STEP

Read off the schema in `crates/fiddle-cli/src/config.rs` at HEAD.

  - `[jira.filing]` in `fiddle.toml`, naming `project`, `issue_type` and
    `ledger_issue`. A deployment that names no such table files nothing, writes
    `{"filing": "not_configured"}` into `filings.json`, and sends no request. The
    run still succeeds. A host that removes the mapping step and forgets this
    table stops filing tickets and reports that only in a file nobody reads
    (ADR 080).
  - `[jira]`, the table `[jira.filing]` sits under. It requires `site`,
    `project`, `user` and `token`. `[jira] project` names the project work items
    are read from, and the schema requires it even though a `cve:` invocation
    reads its work items from the stub port.
  - The two variables `[jira] user` and `[jira] token` name, exported on the
    `Run fiddle for CVE remediation` step. That step exports four variables
    today and none of them is Jira's. A deployment that asks to file and exports
    no credential is refused before the run rather than filing nothing on every
    run.
  - The ledger issue. It must exist before the first run and it must live in
    `[jira.filing] project`. fiddle never creates it. A ledger issue the site
    does not hold is named, and no create is sent.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE

`verdicts.json` keeps being written. `write_report` is its only writer, the Slack
step is M9's and still reads it, and the report bundle is retained evidence in
its own right. Removing the verdict mapping step removes a reader of
`verdicts.json` and not its writer.

`non-patchable.json` is no longer written. Its five fields were how a verdict row
reached a ticket, and fiddle now builds the ticket from the verdict row itself.
The create carries the label the `scan` job's JQL closes, so that job closes a
ticket fiddle files exactly as it closes a ticket the Jira step files.

`legacy_label` stays in `verdicts.json`. ADR 038 gave that field the end date
"when M5 owns the Jira step", and the date has not arrived. fiddle owns the
create and the host still owns the close, and the close is a JQL on
`cve-upstream-blocked` and `cve-needs-work` in the `scan` job, which this patch
does not touch. The field is what fiddle builds its own label from.

